### What is SchildiChat Revenge based on?

SchildiChat Revenge's UI is for the most part written from scratch using Kotlin and Jetpack Compose, while occasionally re-using some components found in SchildiChat Next.
On the data layer, Revenge builds on the [matrix-rust-sdk](https://github.com/SchildiChat/matrix-rust-sdk) and some abstraction layers found in SchildiChat Next / Element&nbsp;X Android.

### How about Web / iOS support?

Technically it could be possible: Kotlin, Compose Multiplatform and Rust all should support these as targets.
However our codebase currently has quite a few JVM dependencies still, and FFI (Rust to Kotlin bindings) needs to be figured out for each platform, so it will probably not be a quick port.
At the current time I have no plans of supporting these, but feel to have a try of porting it yourself if you're interested.

### Can I trigger slash commands from the message composer?

No. Revenge intentionally has a "command mode" that is separate from the message composer,
as this approach allows us to also trigger commands outside of chats, or in chats that don't allow sending messages at all and thus block the composer.
Furthermore, sending messages that start with a slash is not something that the Matrix spec prevents you from doing,
so reserving it for app-side functionality is an unnecessary limitation to what you can send in chats.

### How to enter command mode?

There are multiple ways to enter command mode:

- If you are using a keyboard, you can press control-shift-K (or, if you customized your keybindings, whatever shortcut you have set up for the `Command` command).
- Long-press / right-click any of your chats in inbox and select "Command...". You can do this for both generic commands, or commands specific to the selected chat.
- Long-press / right-click any message and select "Command...". You can do this for generic commands, commands specific to the selected message, or commands specific to the chat you have currently open.
- On desktop, commands that don't require a context can also be passed to an existing SchildiChat instance by invoking SchildiChat from the command line while passing the in-app command with the required parameters (e.g., `schildichat-revenge open inbox` to open a new window showing the room list).

### How to know what commands exist?

Allowing easy discovery for available commands is still on the TODO, so a complete list can currently only be found by [checking out the code](https://github.com/SchildiChat/schildi-revenge/blob/main/config/src/commonMain/kotlin/chat/schildi/revenge/config/keybindings/Action.kt).  
While typing into the command input box, the app will suggest you commands with their required parameters, based on your current context and what you typed so far.
The command input will furthermore before execution indicate commands that look correct with a green background, and commands that look malformed in red.

### What is Schildi taking "Revenge" for?

If you need something specific here, go for the developer pain experienced from maintaining an Element Web fork :)  
The name also alliterates with some core details of this client, being a completely new **Re**write building on the **R**ust SDK.
